{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain \qc\sl240\slmult0\nowidctlpar\adjustright \fs20\lang2057\cgrid {\b\f1\fs36\ul PsiGar 2.9}{\b\f1\fs36 
\par }\pard \sl240\slmult0\nowidctlpar\adjustright {\f1 
\par 
\par 
\par PsiGar is a shareware program for owners of Garmin GPS45 GPS receivers.
\par I have no reports of problems from users with the 38, 40, 45, 65 and 75 models.
\par It will probably work with other Garmin receivers but I can't verify this.
\par There have been some problems with a GPS90, but all the others work ok so far.
\par The problems seem to be with the built in waypoints making route editing impossible.
\par 
\par 
\par If you like this program and/or use it for more than 30 days you are required to register it. The registration details are given below.
\par 
\par 
\par Please read this entire document before using the program.
\par 
\par PsiGar allows you to save Waypoints, Routes, Track Logs and Almanac information
\par onto your Psion series 3a.
\par 
\par The waypoint file can be converted to database compatible file and edited or
\par added to as any other database file. The altered file can then be sent back to the GPS receiver.
\par 
\par The registered version also allows you to send  Routes, Track Logs and Almanac data to the receiver. Of these file types only routes and track logs can be edited.
\par 
\par 
\par The Psiway addon module allows you to enter waypoints in a more convenient way.
\par You can use the Psion to create a waypoint averaged out over a number of  readings, create a waypoint using the Psion as an external keyboard for the GPS and enter waypoints manually and either send them direct to the GPS or save them in a waypoint file.

\par The Psiway addon module also includes Wayedit which supports the direct editing
\par of ".way" files without having to create database files first.
\par You can now enter OSGB grid references directly in Psiway.
\par 
\par When viewing a waypoint file with Psiway you can also view a waypoint in OSGB grid references by selection "OSGB Grid View". You can now edit a waypoint using OSGB Grid references. 
\par 
\par 
\par This software is under constant development so it is quite possible that there could be bugs present in the software.
\par In particular the Psiway module could easily contain bugs as it has had very limited testing.
\par The OSGB bits of Psiway are not yet complete and can crash if you enter invalid numbers, I'm not sure what the valid limits are so I have not yet been able to put constraints into the program
\par 
\par Please read the disclaimer at the end of this document before using the software.
\par 
\par Please feel free to contact me with any suggestions or queries, preferably at the email address below.\page }{\b\f1\fs24 What\rquote s New in Version 2.9?}{\f1 
\par 
\par The installation instructions have changed from versions earlier than 2.7 to make them more compatible with other Psion Applications. The .RSC files are now stored in an APP\\PSIGAR directory rather than an APP directory.
\par 
\par A new option PCX5 has been added to the Special menu. This currently allows conversion from Psigar Waypoint, Route and Track Log files to Garmin PCX5 type files. The Garmin files are by default stored in the PSIGAR\\
PCX5 directory as some of the filename extensions are the same as those used by Psigar. When transferred to a PC these files can be used as standard Garmin PCX5 files. A \ldblquote Fugawi\rdblquote 
 export option has been added to the PCX5 routine to allow exporting of track logs and waypoints to this PC based mapping software.
\par 
\par The GPS communication routines have been rewritten to automatically time out if communication with the GPS is not established within five seconds. This stops you having to \ldblquote kill\rdblquote 
 PsiGar if a GPS communications error occurs. The new routines also transfer waypoints about 50% faster than the old ones.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par \page }{\b\f1\fs24 Installation.
\par }{\f1 
\par Firstly if you are updating from an earlier version than 2.3 you may delete the file "garhead.dbf" from the "m:\\opd" directory.
\par I have changed waypoint database files to use the extention ".wbf" rather than ".dbf" to make them easier to recognise.
\par In version 2.0 and 2.1 Psirbf.als and psitbf.als were incorrectly named psirte.als and psitrk.als. You can rename these files or not as you wish.
\par 
\par 
\par copy PSIGAR28.OPA to your \\APP directory
\par copy PSIWAY.RSC to any \\APP\\PSIGAR directory (you may have to create the directory first)
\par copy PSICON.RSC to any \\APP\\PSIGAR directory
\par copy WAYHEAD.WBF to any \\OPD directory 
\par copy RTEHEAD.RBF to any \\OPD directory
\par copy TRKHEAD.TBF to any \\OPD directory
\par      (You may need to make this directory first)
\par PSIGAR27.OPA is the main program file.
\par PSIWAY.RSC is the PsiWay addon module.
\par PSICON.RSC contains the conversion routines
\par WAYHEAD.WBF is a header file which must be present to edit data files.
\par RTEHEAD.RBF is a header file which must be present to edit routes.
\par TRKHEAD.TBF is a header file which must be present to edit track logs
\par 
\par Optional.
\par copy PSIRBF.ALS to your \\APP directory
\par copy PSITBF.ALS to your \\APP directory
\par copy PSIWBF.ALS to your \\APP directory
\par 
\par These are alias files using the data application to point directly to the route, track and waypoint files in the psigar directory. This speeds up viewing PsiGar database files as you don't have to change directory to access them.
\par 
\par 
\par Install PSIGAR using <PSION> <I> in the usual way
\par 
\par Optional.
\par Install PSIRBF.ALS, PSITBF.ALS and PSIWBF.ALS using <PSION><I>.
\par \page }{\b\f1\fs24 Connecting to a GPS
\par }{\f1 
\par You will need a Psion serial link lead and connection lead to attach to the port on the GPS receiver. The relevant lead with bare wires 
is available from most Garmin dealers. You will probably have to make up the Psion end to connect to the serial link yourself.
\par 
\par For a GPS 38,40,45 or 75 the wiring required is For a 25 way male Dtype as used with the PC 3Link the wires are 
\par pin 2 brown (Data out from GPS)
\par pin 3 white (Data in to GPS)
\par pin 7 black (Ground)
\par 
\par For a 9 way male Dtype as used with the PC 3Link the wires are
\par pin 3 brown (Data out from GPS)
\par pin 2 white (Data in to GPS)
\par pin 5 black (Ground)
\par 
\par If using a Psion modem link rather than a PC link you reverse the wiring for pins 2 an 3 for both 9 and 25 pin dtypes.
\par 
\par The red wire is the external power supply connection and is not needed.
\par If using an external power supply the black wire is the earth for this as well.
\par Some dealers will supply a ready made lead ending with a 3Link mini din plug though this is more expensive, roughly \'a340.
\par 
\par For  a GPS 120 using a Garmin bare wire power data cable
\par GPS 120        25 pin dtype connector
\par Blue      \tab pin 2
\par Brown          \tab pin 3
\par Black          \tab pin 7
\par 
\par You can make the cable up using a mini din plug to connect to the podule on the serial link lead but they can be very difficult to solder due to the number of small pins very close together.\page }{\b\f1\fs24 Operation}{\f1 
\par 
\par The program is menu driven and easy to understand.
\par 
\par 
\par First setup your GPS45 for GARMIN / GARMIN  HOST mode on the I/O setup menu.
\par Make sure the lead is connected and the GPS left on.
\par Make sure the Psion serial link is turned OFF on the system screen.
\par 
\par Run PsiGar.
\par 
\par Select menu GARMIN > PSION and any of the options.
\par 
\par ALL sends almanac info, routes, track logs and waypoints to the Psion.
\par The other options send the indicated items only.
\par 
\par The following default filenames are used:
\par 
\par almanac info        \tab M:\\PSIGAR\\PSIGAR.ALM
\par routes              \tab \tab M:\\PSIGAR\\PSIGAR.RTE
\par track logs          \tab M:\\PSIGAR\\PSIGAR.TRK
\par waypoints           \tab M:\\PSIGAR\\PSIGAR.WAY
\par Waypoint database   \tab M:\\PSIGAR\\PSIGAR.WBF
\par Routes database     \tab M:\\PSIGAR\\PSIGAR.RBF
\par Track Log Database  \tab M:\\PSIGAR\\PSIGAR.TBF
\par 
\par Psiway waypoints    \tab M:\\PSIGAR\\PSIWAY.WAY
\par 
\par 
\par If you now select PSION > GARMIN you can send any or all of these files back to the GPS receiver. (Only waypoints can be sent back in the unregistered version).
\par 
\par The action here is essentially additive for routes, waypoints and track logs.
\par 
\par Sending an almanac overwrites the current one. I can't think why you would want to do this unless your batteries had died and you had lost all your stored data.
\par 
\par Sending a route file updates the routes in the file only, other r
outes are not touched. NOTE If the route you send has names corresponding to existing waypoints these waypoints will be updated if necessary to match the ones in the route. Any waypoints in the route not currently in the waypoint list in the receiver will
 be added to the waypoint list.
\par 
\par Sending track logs updates the track log already present by adding more points.
\par Note a line will be drawn from the last existing point to the first loaded point when viewed on the map screen.
\par I have found it useful to save all my track logs as separate files so that I can retrace any route simply by loading the appropriate log. Having only one route present at any time gives a much clearer display on the map. It also allows you to use a more f
requent update interval on the mapping screen as you can have the full number of points available each time.
\par 
\par Sending waypoints updates any existing waypoints with the same names and adds any that do not exist. Any existing waypoints not in the file will not be touched.
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b\f1\fs24 File Conversion
\par }{\f1 
\par WHEN EDITING ANY DATABASE FILES PLEASE COMPRESS THEM BEFORE SAVING THEM. This avoids occasional errors when converting them back to Garmin files.
\par 
\par You can use the GARMIN Waypoint> DATABASE option under the FILE CONVERSION 
\par menu to convert a Garmin waypoint file to a file that can be edited using the 3a's data program.
\par With PsiGar version 2 I would recommend using the Wayedit option in the PsiWay module instead as it allows for easier text entry with a degree of error checking and also saves the need of converting to database files and back again after editing.
\par 
\par This file will default to M:\\PSIGAR\\PSIGAR.WBF.
\par When entering or altering information using the data program you must stick to the following guidelines. 
\par 
\par 1: The name field should be a max of 6 letters.
\par 2: Latitude should be -89.99998 to 89.99998 degrees positive value for north, negative for south.
\par 3: Longitude should be -179.99998 to 179.99998 degrees positive values for east negative for west.
\par    
\par 4: Latitude and longitude values should be for a WGS84 map datum as this is how the Garmin Protocol outputs its waypoints.
\par    Also if you want to compare latitude and longitude values with those on the gps to check that all is ok, the gps must be set to position format hddd.
ddddd_ on the navigation setup menu. This is not necessary for transfers it is purely for comparison purposes.
\par 
\par 5: The creation date should follow the template DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM:SS
\par \tab    DD=day
\par \tab    MM=month
\par \tab    YYYY=year
\par \tab    HH=hour
\par \tab    MM=minute
\par \tab    SS=second
\par    
\par    Alternatively enter NOW and the current date and time will be used when the file is converted to a Garmin file.
\par    
\par 6: The comment field should be a max of 16 characters (Garmin's protocol seems to suggest a 40 character field but the G
PS45 only seems to handle 16, Other units with larger screens may handle more). If you enter more than 16 characters the GPS will ignore the extra characters.
\par 
\par 7: Do not leave any field blank as this can corrupt the data fields when stored.
\par 
\par As these items
 are being edited using the built in data program no error checking is carried out at entry time. I have included some error trapping in the main program but I'm sure I haven't caught all possible errors. If the GPS doesn't accept a particular waypoint it
 will give an error message and not load it, the rest of the file will be loaded as normal.   
\par 
\par 
\par If you want to enter waypoints manually it is much faster and less error prone to use the Psiway module.
\par 
\par 
\par When you have finished altering the data close the file.
\par You can now use the DATABASE WAYPOINT> GARMIN option in the FILE CONVERSION 
\par menu to convert the database file back to a Garmin file in preparation for sending it back to the GPS. I would suggest using a new filename here in case you have messed up!
\par 
\par When you send data back to the GPS it seems to just ignore incorrect data or give a waypoint error message.
\par 
\par If you want to send almanac info, routes or track logs back to the GPS you will need to register the program.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par Editing of routes is now available in the same manner as editing waypoints.
\par 
\par As this part of the program is new it has not undergone the degree of soak testing that the other routines have.
\par 
\par First you save get both route and waypoint files from the Garmin. The waypoint file is required as you need a file containing all available waypoints. This is to allow you to add additional route waypoints to thos stored in the routes.
\par 
\par Second you convert a Garmin route file to a Database route file using the GARMIN ROUTES > DATABASE option under the FILE CONVERSION Menu.
\par This gives you a file which can be edited using the Data program.
\par 
\par A record in this file must stick to the following format
\par 
\par 
\par Route number    X% (0-19)
\par Route Name      XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX (max 16 characters)
\par Route Waypoints XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX (Max 5 names of 6 chrs)
\par Route Waypoints XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX (Max 5 names of 6 chrs)
\par Route Waypoints XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX (Max 5 names of 6 chrs)
\par Route Waypoints XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX (Max 5 names of 6 chrs)
\par Route Waypoints XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX (Max 5 names of 6 chrs)
\par Route Waypoints XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX,XXXXXX (Max 5 names of 6 chrs)
\par 
\par You can have 20 routes of up to 30 waypoints each.
\par 
\par Note the route name will be shown as ________________ if the GPS has derived its name from your waypoints, rather than a manually entered route name.
\par 
\par The waypoint names must be identical to the wa
ypoint names stored in the Garmin Waypoint file. If you want to use waypoints from a selection of different waypoint files, copy all the files to the Garmin first and then save the combined file back to the Psion. Use this combined file when asked for the
 waypoint file.
\par 
\par The order of the routes within the Database file is not important.
\par 
\par When you have finished editing the routes save the file and exit the data program.
\par 
\par Use the DATABASE ROUTES > GARMIN option under the FILE CONVERSION menu to 
\par compile an appropriate file to send back to the Garmin.
\par You will need to specify the database file, A waypoint file containing all the Waypoints used in all the routes and a file to save the output to.
\par  
\par This option is quite slow if you are dealing with a large number of waypoints and routes. 
\par 
\par 
\par Editing Track Logs is almost identical to editing Waypoints. There are a couple of things to note.
\par Firstly the track point must be a 4 digit number ie point 1 is 0001 not 1.
\par Secondly when converting back to Garmin T
rack Log Files, the process can be very slow as the database must be sorted to get the track points into ascending order first. The sorting routine is now much faster than in previous versions, (thanks to someone who anonymously sent me a better sort rout
ine!). 
\par You can also now use an external sort routine if you prefer, the program now checks if the file is in order before attempting to sort it. If the file is already sorted the sort routine does not run.
\par 
\par There appears to be a problem in how Garmin GPS's
 handle dates in track logs, when sending a track log back to the GPS all date and time information is set back to the GPS default. This means that if you send the track back to the psion again all the dates appear as 31/12/1989 00:00:00. This is not a bu
g in this software, all software that sends tracks to the GPS has the same problem.
\par The field marked start point dictates how a track log is split up. If this field is set to 1 the point will be taken as the start of a new track and will not be joined on th
e GPS screen to the previous point. You can actually use this to fix the breaks that occur in a track log due to "poor GPS coverage"
\par problems causing an artificial break. Just turn the break point back to 0 and the track will join up across the gap.
\par 
\par The S
plit Track Database function gives you a convenient way of picking out a section of a track log to keep on its own. E.g. say you had two different journeys stored in one track log file. You can look at the database file and note where each journey starts 
and ends, and then use the split function to save them as separate files.
\par 
\par 
\par Under the special menu there is now an option to allow you to set the time on the Psion to match the Garmin's time. This has been updated to allow you to give a local time offset from Greenwich mean Time.\page }{\b\f1\fs24 Troubleshooting}{\f1 
\par 
\par The  most likely cause of trouble is the GPS not being set for the correct mode of operation or the lead being incorrectly wired. The program will hang up if it cannot maintain communication with the GPS receiver. T
here is nothing I can do about this, it is a function of how the Psion handles its serial interface - it only returns control to the program when the requested number of bytes have been transferred, if not enough bytes are transferred it never returns con
trol. Under these situations the only way to exit the program is to return to the system menu and kill the program using <Psion> <shift> <K>.
\par 
\par 
\par I have noticed that when you have finished uploading to the GPS it is usually necessary to switch it off and back
 on again to get the nearest waypoints displayed in the moving map screen. It is probably a good idea to always switch the GPS off and on again anyway after uploading data to it.
\par 
\par 
\par There is a potential problem when converting database routes back to Garmin
 routes. The system memory usage of this function is right on the limit on my Psion. If anyone gets "No system memory" errors when using this function please let me know. I could fix this but it would render the function unavailable to people using GPS 90
's for example which need more memory to store waypoints.\page }{\b\f1\fs24 PSIWAY}{\f1 
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par Psiway lets you create waypoints in several new ways.
\par 
\par Average GPS Waypoint.
\par 
\par This allows you to create a waypoint based on the average of several different readings taken over a period of time, to hopefully even out any errors.
\par You specify how many readings to take and at what time intervals. When completed you specify what to call the waypoint, give it a description and it is then sent automatically to the GPS. The description defaults to the Psion's current time and date.

\par 
\par 
\par Auto GPS Waypoint.
\par 
\par In this mode the Psion acts as an external keyboard for the GPS. When you select this option the Psion gets a position fix from the GPS and lets you enter a name and description. The waypoint is then sent to the GPS. 
\par 
\par Manual ddd.ddddd_ Waypoint.
\par 
\par As auto GPS Waypoint except the latitude and longitude are specified by you rather than read from the GPS. When completed the waypoint is sent directly to the GPS.
\par 
\par Manual ddd_mm.mmm' Waypoint.
\par 
\par This is as the Manual ddd.ddddd waypoint except the latitude and longitude are entered in degrees and minutes (on two separate lines). This is often easier when entering waypoints from a map.
\par Note negative values represent south and west as normal. The negative sign is only entered for the degrees not the minutes.
\par 
\par Manual OSGB Waypoint.
\par 
\par This allows you to enter a waypoint using OSGB Grid references or eastings/northings. 
\par 
\par 
\par Wayedit.
\par 
\par These options allow you to directly edit a .way file either using ddd.ddddd_ or ddd_mm.mmm' for latitude and longitude entry.
\par I have limited the accuracy to 5 decimal places to match the values displayed on the GPS, this is equivalent to about 1 metre anyway. I would love to see a map that could be read to this degree of accuracy!
\par 
\par You can now also edit waypoint files in OSGB coo
rdinates, but it is very slow to change waypoints, the on the fly conversions are a bit sore on the Psion's brain! Only use this mode if you want to enter waypoints from a map. If you simply want to rename waypoints use one of the other options instead.

\par 
\par You can edit an existing ".way" file or start from scratch.
\par 
\par These options use the same text entry modes as for the manual waypoints.
\par The difference is that these options store the waypoints in a file rather than sending them directly to the GPS. This lets 
you work in isolation from the GPS entering several waypoints from a map for instance and then send the complete file to the GPS at one go.
\par 
\par To move around within the waypoint file you use the "Action" option at the top of the dialog box.
\par You can choose Add, Update, Erase, Search, Previous, Next, First Goto and OSGB Grid View.
\par 
\par Add.
\par You edit the currently displayed waypoint and it is then added to the waypoint file as a new waypoint, leaving the original one unchanged.
\par The creation time will automatically be set to the current psion date and time. If you want to set the description to the current date and time simply enter a description of "NOW", without the quotes.
\par I know it's not as pretty as using a clean screen but it works! It also helps for several close waypoints as you will probably only have to alter the minutes and not the degrees
\par 
\par Update.
\par This will replace the current waypoint with your edited version. As usual it will be stored as the last record in the file. The creation time will be left unchang
ed unless you enter "NOW" as the waypoint description, this will also change the creation time.
\par 
\par Erase.
\par Delete the currently displayed waypoint.
\par 
\par Search.
\par Search for a Waypoint name.
\par 
\par Previous.
\par Step back one waypoint.
\par 
\par Next.
\par Step forward one waypoint.
\par 
\par First.
\par Go directly to the first waypoint.
\par 
\par Goto.
\par Specify a waypoint to goto by number.
\par 
\par OSGB Grid View.
\par View current waypoint in OSGB Grid references.
\par 
\par 
\par \page }{\b\f1\fs24 PCX5.}{\f1 
\par 
\par This new option under the Special menu allows you to convert routes, track logs and waypoint Psigar files to the equivalent Garmin PCX5 files.
\par 
\par Only waypoints may be converted in the unregistered version.
\par 
\par This is a new option and is very much in an alpha test state so use with care!
\par 
\par The converted files will by default be stored in a \\psigar\\pcx5 directory. This is because the track log .trk and route .rte extensions required by PCX5 are the same as those used by PsiGar.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par \page 
